WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:12.655
KAMENA KOSTOVA: My name
is Kamena Kostova.

00:00:12.655 --> 00:00:14.510
And I'm originally
from Bulgaria.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.460
And I'm a senior at MIT.

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.840
I'm majoring in biology.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.595
And next year, I'm going to UCSF
to do a Ph.D. In biology.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.000
The reason I came
to MIT because I

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.270
wanted to do research.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.240
And I knew that the biology
department at MIT is one of

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.040
the best departments.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.700
So I actually started applying
for Europe before I came to

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:38.950
the States.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:51.550
I started working in Professor
Jacks' lab my freshman year.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.020
It was very hard at the
beginning because I didn't

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.720
have experience.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.262
And probably my English
was extremely poor.

00:00:59.262 --> 00:01:00.880
DAVID FELDSER: Is
that link p21?

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:01.780
Or is that--

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.020
KAMENA KOSTOVA: She's working
like link p21, right?

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:06.510
As an undergraduate,
I should be always

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:07.770
supervised by someone.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.680
And it's definitely a good idea
having someone around in

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.800
lab when you're working
with chemicals.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.760
DAVID FELDSER: When Kamena came
to our laboratory, we

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.330
were interested in
a specific tumor

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:19.660
suppressor gene called p53.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.770
And we were trying to understand
how p53 works in a

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.210
prevalent cancer type called
lung adenocarcinoma.

00:01:29.966 --> 00:01:33.310
KAMENA KOSTOVA: I enjoy
doing research.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.850
But I also enjoyed the fact
that my research can have

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.540
impact on society and other
people's lives.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:46.470
The goals of the lab is to build
mass models of cancer.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:48.990
So we're trying to recapitulate

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.470
cancer in the mouse.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:55.020
And use a mouse as the
tool to study cancer.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.620
So what we're interested
in is p53.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:00.620
And this is a tumor
suppressor gene.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.030
The reason why it's called a
tumor suppressor gene is

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.180
because it helps your
body against cancer.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.760
So if a cell decides to become
cancerous and starts dividing

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:13.680
and accumulates mutation, this
gene gets activated and tells

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.525
the cell to stop and correct
these mistakes.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.200
When the cells become cancerous,
very often the

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.720
cells commit suicide.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.520
They accumulate mistakes and
are about to pass the

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.610
threshold of malignancy, and
then they decide to kill

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:29.040
themselves.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.000
Because it's better
for the body if it

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.600
loses these bad cells.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.670
I think it's fascinating that
a cell in your pinkie thinks

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:40.880
about the cells in your brain
and what's good for them.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:44.200
The project that I'm currently
working on is a drug screen.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.240
So I'm looking for small
molecules that can restore the

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.080
function of this tumor
suppressor gene in vivo and be

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.301
used as the anticancer
therapeutics.

00:02:55.301 --> 00:02:57.490
DAVID FELDSER: We are in
an academic setting.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.690
And I think it's important to
train young people who want to

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.150
learn how to do science.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:07.310
And having access to MIT
undergraduates in particular

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:11.534
is a real benefit to carry
out the research.

00:03:11.534 --> 00:03:14.045
KAMENA KOSTOVA: By using
microscopy, we can look at

00:03:14.045 --> 00:03:16.960
very thin slices of tissues.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.960
We stain for different pathways
different proteins

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:20.960
that get expressed.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.410
We look at different
interaction

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.015
between cell types--

00:03:24.015 --> 00:03:26.930
look how the tumor progresses
based on the

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.420
morphology of the cells.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.330
What I learn here is how to
be a good scientist--

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.820
how to ask the right questions,
how to design

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.000
experiments, how to look
critically at your data, how

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.020
to communicate with your peers,
and by doing research.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.200
And Professor Jacks' lab really
convinced me that I

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.635
want to stay in academia.

00:03:45.635 --> 00:03:46.930
I really want to be a

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:48.180
biologist and to
be a scientist.

